model {
  extend basalt {
    pool = module "Pool app" {
      #basalt #v1 #deep
      description "Pools select reusable saved accounts connected on provider apps. The new source contains no Subscription acquisition declaration, sign-in hooks or login-file tool. Retained Subscription links keep their exact old active source and provenance until approved adoption or explicit removal leaves zero legacy links; the host guards default and manual source activation. Worker acquisition routes refuse before state or provider work, while cleanup never finalizes a stale acquisition and legacy reads, checks, routing and removal remain supported. One private app instance per person per organization, run by the tools service. The pool owner's membership-scoped Home readings feed the host's generic allowance observations, preserving reported windows, quantities, periods, source times and expiring balances. Observation endpoints remain owner-only; members keep the existing health path. The banked-reset browser fixture seeds all journey credits before account acquisition can trigger its first observation, and confirms that exact initial count before spending. Hosted reset actions take a saved account and explicit pool, require current account authority and that pool's owner, and keep native or retained subscription backend references on the server. Browser spending rejects API credentials; agent spending requires framework approval. Fresh catalog and receipt reads never spend or run follow-ups. The owner can read the latest durable attempt after losing a reply or client draft; that read preserves its original credit and request ID, result and retry deadline without running provider or follow-up work. Unknown attempts remain blocked after the provider retry deadline. Accounts uses the host's native check status with exception-only pool badges; idle freshness, zero reset counts and internal window IDs are omitted. The native subscription health response omits identity when the label is the account's own id, preserving health and usage without exposing the host's reference. A lab's identity colour comes from one shared mapping, used by provider icons, healthy usage bars and pool usage chart series; low, critical and stale readings keep their status colours, and a stale reading names itself. Every selected member stays in live and retained pool views, including app API keys and the control account. A retained quota reading keeps its original freshness until the earliest reported account or displayed-window deadline; missing deadlines and expired readings remain stale without changing their observation times. The backend separately classifies subscription member IDs from the served catalog; overview counts, quota freshness and capacity consume that classification without changing selections. Capacity resolves vendor plan identities once for host labels, supported windows and sizing. Fresh account absolute limits outrank bounded estimates from matched quota and request intervals, a maintained subscription dataset, and expiring vendor seeds. Every value keeps its source, original check time, verification and expiry. Daily source checks run through the existing alarm, retry unavailable sources separately and revoke changed or missing facts. Only current usable facts can contradict another source; revoked facts cannot suppress a fresh fallback. Unknown plans use a separate one-account unit quietly; published relative units are never guessed from price. Stored observations keep their original values and provenance. Participation summaries distinguish active people from everyone with pool access; their nouns agree with the total. Overview separates measured provider quota windows and preserves missing hours. Account views and capacity sampling normalize the same explicit fixed-duration unit/value fields; missing periods and calendar months never become guessed weekly allowances. Model breakdowns remain separate from physical account capacity. Current catalog facts provide missing supported windows; actual reported windows survive temporary caps and expire independently. A missing current five-hour reading shows an unknown state and its last recorded time when available, without percentages or idle/full claims. Support-only panels contain no invented quantities, history points, refills or forecasts. Its same-origin usage bridge resolves the app and signed-in user through the existing app session authorization and calls the existing hosted series service, preserving management and per-account inspection checks. Missing user sessions refuse; app identity never substitutes for the person. Members retain their own usage view. Forecast advice remains unavailable until comparable weighted demand is established. Max 20x contributes twenty weekly plan units under pool policy, with a distinct policy revision; previously stored readings keep their original weights and provenance. Only the affected weekly demand cohort restarts when that policy changes. A weight revision change restarts only that cohort's demand training after its mixed hour, clearing its incompatible account shares and rolling-use chains while preserving other cohorts' training, rolling state and all displayed observations. Forecast policy provenance survives empty membership; legacy demand without provenance cannot qualify a reconnected account. Before the native-policy handoff the recorded owner retains the existing management rule. People set up and list their computers on the tools gateway's Connect page, which asks the pool Worker for a signed setup intent through this app's computerContext query. The pool screen refreshes its signed header links only while both the host and browser show it, renewing once on an overdue return. App screens embed on isolated opaque same-site origins; live routing acceptance is pending. When its first load succeeds, the pool marks the committed account view, including an authorized empty result, once per mount as basalt:pool-accounts-ready and posts only that name, contract version 2 and a snapshot boolean to the configured host origin. The parent probe accepts only those three fields, a boolean snapshot value, the expected origin and the exact iframe window. A failed first load excludes the document from readiness measurement even after recovery. A snapshot commits before the single fresh follow-up starts. The readiness mark detail and parent boolean describe the same first committed view; legacy views without reading metadata remain live. The rendered manager and personal overviews commit usage independently of capacity and the pool view, preserving each read's authorization and range. Totals, models, failures and retries stay usable while capacity is held. Names fill in without raw provider IDs, premature accounts-ready signals or another usage read. Capacity verdicts wait for provider names and matching successful results from one refresh. Incomplete usage coverage keeps its warning without withholding an independently measured capacity verdict; mismatched ranges or capture watermarks still withhold it. Capacity covers only labs with a current pool account; readings retained after an account is removed never read as current capacity. The browser contract fixture serves both overview and capacity responses, identifies overview reads separately, and targets each retry within the failed view it recovers. Document-load readiness and usage readiness remain separate. Public USD estimates retain successful refreshes and fall back to a shipped dated models.dev snapshot across first-read failures. The whole Home preview budget exceeds its source-fetch budget; failures retain a closed error class and HTTP status without backend bodies. History readers refuse new HTTP reads while their frame or browser tab is inactive and abort pending discovery, bounded-page and grouped-series reads on hiding. Returning to the view resynchronizes through the existing revision feed before publishing. History groups use the same request-level USD calculation without changing retained Home charges or import valuations. The pool Worker answers GET /pool/v1/release with its code revision, a hash of its local sources generated at preparation, and its signing-key version, without waking the container."
      metadata { code "apps/pool"; libraries "apps|react|react-dom|@sqlite.org/sqlite-wasm"; bindings "ctx=basalt.router" }
    }
  }
}
